[include version_include.man]
[manpage_begin ns_module n [vset version]]
[moddesc {NaviServer Built-in Commands}]
[titledesc {Module context, metadata, Tcl dependencies, and module reload}]
[description]
[para]
The [cmd ns_module] command describes the module whose Tcl library is currently
being initialized. NaviServer sources [file init.tcl] before the other Tcl files
in that module's library.

[list_begin definitions]
[call [cmd ns_module] [method require] [opt [option {-module module}]] [opt [arg {file ...}]]]
Called at the top level of a module definition file, ensures that the named
prerequisite files have been sourced before execution continues. Files are
relative to the specified module, or the current module when [option -module]
is omitted. With [option -module] and no filenames, requires all top-level
[const *.tcl] definition files of that module. The module must be configured
for the current virtual server. Circular dependencies raise an error.

Private files override shared files with the same relative name. Each file is
loaded once during startup, including files loaded early through dependencies.
Equivalent relative names, such as [const base.tcl] and [const ./base.tcl],
share one dependency identity. Redundant separators and current-directory
components are normalized before dependency registration and loading.
Absolute paths and parent-directory components are rejected.
[const init.tcl] cannot be required.

[call [cmd ns_module] [method reload] [arg module]]
Re-sources the module's top-level Tcl definition files, plus previously loaded
files in that module and all files transitively dependent on them, including
files in other modules. Prerequisites are sourced before their consumers and
each affected file is sourced once per execution of the loader. Dependencies
are recorded by [method require] and refreshed when files are sourced again.
Whole-module requirements record the definition files present when evaluated.

The operation uses [cmd {ns_eval -sync}] for blueprint publication and normal
interpreter propagation. It waits for publication, not for all active
interpreters to adopt the new blueprint. The result is a list of affected
[const {module file}] pairs; recursive prerequisite loading can change the
actual source order.

Source errors stop the operation and propagate to the caller; the failed
operation does not publish a new blueprint. Module context and dependency
bookkeeping are restored on errors. Arbitrary effects of executed Tcl code,
such as shared-variable changes, cannot be rolled back. Files removed since
loading cause an error rather than silently leaving stale definitions.

[const init.tcl] retains its existing startup behavior and is excluded from
reload. Other Tcl files must be suitable for repeated evaluation. Procedures
removed from a source file are not automatically deleted from interpreters.
Do not register schedules or create process resources in reloadable files
unless the initialization is explicitly safe to repeat. The existing
[cmd ns_eval] implementation can evaluate the loader both in the caller and
in a worker interpreter.


[call [cmd ns_module] [const name] [opt [arg value]]]
Return or set the current module name. NaviServer establishes this context
before sourcing its Tcl library.
[call [cmd ns_module] [const shared] [opt [arg value]]]
Return or set the shared module library directory.
[call [cmd ns_module] [const private] [opt [arg value]]]
Return or set the private, server-specific module library directory.
The [const library] subcommand is an alias for [const private].
[call [cmd ns_module] [const version] [opt [arg value]]]
Return the current module's version, or declare it when [arg value] is supplied.
[call [cmd ns_module] [const tag] [opt [arg value]]]
Return the current module's source revision/build tag, or declare it when
[arg value] is supplied.
[call [cmd ns_module] [const clear]]
Clear the initialization context. This does not remove registered metadata.
[list_end]

[para]
Version and tag declarations require nonempty values and are accepted only during
server startup. Each field can be declared once; repeating a declaration raises
an error, even when the value is identical. Fields already supplied by a binary
module's [const Ns_ModuleGetInfo] callback cannot be replaced. Mixed C/Tcl
modules should use their existing callback metadata.

[para]
Declared fields are retained in the loaded-module registry and returned by
[cmd {ns_server modules}]. Tcl-module metadata is scoped to the virtual server
being initialized. Clearing the context or creating a new interpreter does not
clear these fields. Reading a field that has not been declared returns an empty
string; accessing version or tag without a current module raises an error.

[example_begin]
 # In a Tcl module's init.tcl:
 ns_module version 0.1
 ns_module tag nsmcp-0.1-12-g0123456789ab

 # After startup:
 dict get [lb]ns_server modules[rb] nsmcp
[example_end]

[para]
The module build include [file Makefile.module] can generate an entry point from
[arg TCL_INIT] (a template file), writing [arg TCL_INIT_FILE] (default
[file init.tcl]). Set [arg NS_MODULE_VERSION] to the release version and use
[const @NS_MODULE_VERSION@] and [const @NS_MODULE_TAG@] in the template.
The build tag defaults to [cmd {git describe --abbrev=12 --always --dirty=+}].
An explicit [arg NS_MODULE_TAG] overrides it. Without Git, the generated
[cmd {ns_module tag}] declaration is retained, or [const unknown] is used when
no recorded tag exists. Include the generated entry point in distribution
archives so installation preserves the source identity. The generated file is
added to the shared Tcl files installed by [cmd {make install}].

[example_begin]
 # In a module definition file:
 ns_module require -module mod1 file1.tcl file2.tcl
 ns_module require -module mod2
 ns_module require foo1.tcl foo2.tcl

 # From an administrative interpreter:
 ns_module reload mod1
[example_end]

